    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat, not by making cold. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it absorbs heat from inside the fridge and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. The hot gas travels through coils behind or under the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen. As it cools, the refrigerant turns back into a liquid and cycles around again. This loop keeps repeating, carrying heat out of the fridge. Since heat is constantly removed, the inside stays cold, which slows down bacteria and keeps your food fresh longer."
